# Line-ending policy.
#
# Two rules, and the second one is not cosmetic: a Windows batch file with LF-only
# line endings misbehaves when run, and Install_WSL_Ubuntu.bat was committed that
# way. Because the repository is developed inside WSL (where everything is LF) but
# consumed on Windows, an explicit attribute is the only reliable way to keep this
# correct.
#
# Without these rules a `git add` from WSL will happily rewrite the .bat to LF
# again, silently breaking it.

# Shell and Python scripts must be LF. CRLF in a shebang line makes the script
# unrunnable on Linux with a confusing "bad interpreter" error.
*.sh        text eol=lf
*.py        text eol=lf
*.bash      text eol=lf

# Windows-authored files: stored verbatim (CRLF) in the repository.
#
# `-text` rather than `text eol=crlf`, and the distinction matters. With
# `text eol=crlf` git still normalises the *stored* blob to LF and only restores
# CRLF on checkout, so the repository copy stays LF and any tool that reads the
# blob directly — including a GitHub "Download ZIP" of a single file, or a
# deployment that checks files out with autocrlf disabled — gets a broken .bat.
# `-text` means "this is not a text file, do not touch it", which keeps CRLF in
# the object store, where it belongs for a Windows batch file.
*.bat       -text
*.cmd       -text
*.ps1       text eol=crlf
*.sln       text eol=crlf
*.vcxproj   text eol=crlf

# Text data files: normalise on commit, native on checkout.
*.md        text
*.json      text
*.yml       text
*.yaml      text
*.txt       text
*.env       text

# Never convert these.
*.png       binary
*.jpg       binary
*.jpeg      binary
*.gif       binary
*.webp      binary
*.ico       binary
*.so        binary
*.whl       binary
*.pyc       binary
